--
117-
118-Call that the <Term id="agentic-artifact">**agentic artifact**</Term>: whatever shapes how the agent reasons, acts, accesses tools, applies context, follows policy, or produces work.
119-
120-The runtime executes. A skill shapes its behavior. Markdown instructions don't reason on their own, and a signed context pack doesn't enforce the permissions it describes.
121-
122:Instruction, Tool, Context and Governance are the static side: the things an agent draws on. Execution is the runtime topology that selects which of them a session actually loads, and Evidence is what that session leaves behind.
123-
124-| Artifact family | Examples | What needs versioning and review |
125-| --- | --- | --- |
126-| Instruction | Prompts, skills, commands, rules | Non-executable instructions and any bundled executable dependencies |
127-| Tool | MCP servers, scripts, API adapters | Executable implementation, tool declarations, and permission boundaries |
128-| Context | Context packs, memories, domain references | Non-executable inputs, provenance, freshness, and scope |
129-| Governance | Policies, approvals, evaluation sets | Declared constraints and the mechanisms that enforce or test them |
130-| Execution | Workflows, agent definitions, deployment bindings | The configuration selecting instructions, tools, and runtime conditions |
131:| Evidence | Manifests, run records, verification results | Retained observations and their links to artifacts and accepted work |
132-
133-I use <Term id="agentic-artifact-supply-chain">**agentic artifact supply chain**</Term> for the managed lifecycle of those reusable instructions, context, tools, policies and related assets. Existing work on agent supply chains and governed agent primitives already covers this ground; this is my organizing label for the lifecycle I'm building, not a new supply-chain analogy.[^primitives]
134-
135-Both executable code and non-executable context are versioned dependencies. Changing either can change runtime behavior. Freezing both makes the inputs inspectable; it doesn't freeze the model's reasoning.
136-
--
274-| Responsibility | What it owes the reviewer workflow |
275-| --- | --- |
276-| **Supply chain** | Artifact identity, dependencies, recorded deployment, promotion, replacement, and revocation |
277-| **Runtime control** | Caller identity, current authorization, tool permissions, budgets, and enforcement during use |
278-| **Orchestration** | Routing the review, carrying its scope across handoffs, retries, and human decision points |
279:| **Evidence** | What ran, what it produced, what was verified, and who accepted it |
280-
281-Four responsibilities, not four products, and they overlap: context is a governed dependency and an input to a runtime decision at once. Writing a policy under one of these headings doesn't establish that a runtime checks it, so I still need to name the mechanism in the execution path and what it blocks. My own <Term id="aos">Agentic OS (AOS)</Term> covers these unevenly. The table is the obligation, not a claim of coverage; they become a control fabric only once the handoffs carry identity and evidence.
282-
283-Shared identity means the deployment record and run record can refer to the same exact artifact. Shared evidence means the next system can inspect what the previous one established. An explicit handoff says who checks the next condition and what happens when it fails.
284-
--
372-**Proposed:** complete cross-system linkage from loaded artifact version to accepted outcome. Local checks are implemented; fleet-wide compliance remains a target state in the registry's own August 11 rules.[^coverage]
373-
374-**Proposed:** controlled comparisons of pinned artifacts and context are how I intend to test the broader claim that these mechanisms improve outcomes.
375-</Callout>
376-
377:## Receipts behind the argument
378-
379-These are dated implementation records, summarized as methods. The August commits establish when checks entered the repository; the September audit establishes what that later inspection found. Neither date silently becomes the incident date or an attestation of today's runtime state.
380-
381:<Evidence kind="observed" /> **The reviewer copy.** The drift-check source records a deployed reviewer missing its mandatory establish-the-diff step while the upstream copy contained it. The incident was filed August 5; the check was committed August 6. Its five-day interval describes the stale deployment. Comparing deployed bytes with the source makes that discrepancy visible, but doesn't recover a per-session loading history or count incorrect reviews.[^drift]
382-
383:<Evidence kind="observed" /> **Sibling instructions.** One harness-specific instruction file carried superseded doctrine for six weeks after its canonical sibling changed. The parity gate committed August 5 checks a declared relationship between those files. It doesn't require every document to be identical. The separate live-document staleness check catches a named obsolete assertion outside that pair; it leaves dated historical records alone.[^parity][^staleness]
384-
385:<Evidence kind="observed" /> **Workflow copies that needed different repairs.** The synchronization source records both missing upstream review checks and local copies with stricter conditional-verdict handling. The August 11 implementation preserves per-project rosters and refuses local-ahead overwrites. That refusal protects the difference from erasure; it doesn't certify the difference as correct or promote it into shared policy.[^workflow]
386-
387:<Evidence kind="measured" /> **Ancestry versus released content.** The retained maintenance record reports negative commit-ancestry and tag-containment checks, followed by direct inspection of the release's file, which found the fix. The repository's squash-merge history explains the discrepancy. This measures presence in the tagged source, not presence in a deployed process, and supplies no outcome comparison.[^ancestry]
388-
389:<Evidence kind="measured" /> **A provenance writer on one path.** The September 16 audit traced automatic BOM emission through composite/bundle materialization and inspected the separate single-artifact deployment route. The latter emitted activity events without invoking the BOM generator. The finding is a path-coverage gap. It doesn't measure the proportion of all deployments missing records, and a working bundle path doesn't close that gap.[^bom-scope]
390-
391:<Evidence kind="measured" /> **Real records, unresolved caller.** The same audit inspected three real context-provenance rows with deployment findings and traced the CLI's call into the writer. It found no durable dispatch log in the inspected canonical checkout that identified which actor produced those rows. That leaves the actor unresolved. It doesn't make the rows fictitious, and it doesn't prove the automated pilot produced them.[^context-records]
392-
393:<Evidence kind="observed" /> The compact chronology below comes from the first commits of the named local mechanisms. These are implementation-history markers, not public-priority claims or dates of complete fleet coverage.
394-
395-| First committed | Local mechanism | What the record adds |
396-| --- | --- | --- |
397-| June 2026 | Upstream artifact registry | A declared source for shared artifacts |
398-| August 2026 | Sibling-instruction parity check | A check on a named consumer relationship |
399-| August 2026 | Deployed-artifact drift check | Comparison at the deployment destination |
400-| August 2026 | Direction-aware workflow synchronization | Protection against erasing local-ahead changes |
401-
402:<Evidence kind="proposed" /> Those repairs support the managed-variation architecture in this essay. They don't establish a complete identity-to-outcome chain. I still need evidence for when a local improvement should become shared policy, when an automated fast path should return to judgment, and how an independent acceptance decision changes results. The three-project comparison is illustrative. No promotion, demotion or acceptance-by-assertion incident is being claimed from these receipts.
403-
404:<Evidence kind="external" /> The four vendor descriptions and the related architecture research are outside sources. Their product boundaries and contribution claims remain distinct from my implementation records. In particular, no external benchmark result is reported here as a benefit measured in the lab.
405-
406:<Callout type="info" title="Evidence labels">
407:<EvidenceLegend />
408-
409-This essay reports no measured outcome improvement.
410-</Callout>
