src/pages/start-here.astro-63-    <!-- What this site is -->
src/pages/start-here.astro-64-    <section>
src/pages/start-here.astro-65-      <h2>What is Signal to System?</h2>
src/pages/start-here.astro-66-      <p>
src/pages/start-here.astro:67:        Signal to System is a technical publication by Nick Miethe, focused on the intersection of AI agents, agentic software delivery, architecture, platform engineering, and technical leadership. Each piece is written for practitioners with real stakes in these systems — not spectators.
src/pages/start-here.astro-68-      </p>
src/pages/start-here.astro-69-      <p>
src/pages/start-here.astro-70-        The name reflects a core belief: signal always precedes system. Understanding the underlying pattern — the signal — is what allows you to build systems that are durable, comprehensible, and fit for purpose. This publication tries to surface the signal.
src/pages/start-here.astro-71-      </p>
--
src/pages/about.astro-27-      <p>
src/pages/about.astro-28-        My path here started in network engineering consulting (Cisco), then software engineering (SAS, Allscripts), and then moved through enterprise container enablement, automation transformation, and eventually into the consulting-leadership layer where architecture decisions meet go-to-market reality. That sequence produced a systems-first bias I haven't been able to shake: I treat "platform," "process," and "people" as three views of the same problem, not three separate workstreams. I also have a long history of internal tools development and optimization; I'm perpetually unable to operate with inefficiencies.
src/pages/about.astro-29-      </p>
src/pages/about.astro-30-      <p>
src/pages/about.astro:31:        Outside client work, I am a hobby developer of software, which is where tools like <strong>CCDash</strong> (session analytics for agent work) and <strong>SkillMeat</strong> (an artifact manager for agentic development) live. These aren't weekend toys; they're the places I test ideas before they show up in client engagements or on this site. I also speak occasionally on OpenShift and platform topics (Red Hat Summit Connect, KubeCon), and right now I'm particularly focused on AI agents as genuine participants in software delivery: not AI-as-autocomplete, but AI as a structural force that changes what accountability, seniority, and SDLC architecture actually mean.
src/pages/about.astro-32-      </p>
src/pages/about.astro-33-    </section>
